        h1 Changelog
        link RSS /rss.xml
      text Latest updates and announcements.
      h2 October 2026 - Trace, Code Block and AI Elements
      text Today, we are releasing two new components,  and , and , a set of patterns for agentic interfaces built from shadcn/ui components.
      row
        link Trace /docs/components/base/trace
        link "Code Block" /docs/components/base/code-block
        link "AI Elements" /docs/ai-elements
      text Trace and Code Block are available for Base UI, React Aria and Radix across all eight styles.
      h2 Trace#
      text Trace is a list of lines for the steps an agent takes. We built it by studying hundreds of components from agentic interfaces and distilling what they share into one composition you can reuse.
      card
        text Why does pnpm install fail in CI?
        button "Thought for 5s"
        button "Ran pnpm --version"
        button "Ran git diff --stat HEAD~1 -- package.json pnpm-lock.yaml"
        card
          text package.json | 2 +- 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)
        button "Failed to run pnpm install --frozen-lockfile"
        button "Used 2 sources"
        text The last commit bumped a version in package.json without updating pnpm-lock.yaml, so the frozen install fails. Run pnpm install locally and commit the lockfile.
        button Replay
        text "\"use client\" import * as React from \"react\""
        button "View Code"
      text Reasoning, tool calls, sources, plans and subagent runs all have the same shape: something the agent did, and what happened. In Trace, each one is a line with an icon, a title, and an optional description and status. A line can expand to show more detail, link to a page, or open a dialog or sheet, so every step looks and behaves the same in your interface.
      text running, error and pending states, with a shimmer while a step runs.
      text Lines that expand, link out, or open a dialog, sheet, drawer or popover.
      text Nested lines, so a summary expands into steps and a step into its output.
      text Works with the reasoning, tool and source parts from the AI SDK.
      text Keyboard and screen reader support from the trigger that wraps each line.
      card
        row
          tabs
            tab *pnpm
            tab npm
            tab yarn
            tab bun
        button Copy
      h2 Code Block#
      text CodeBlock displays code, terminal output, logs and diffs. It has a header with a title and actions, line numbers, wrapping, diff and highlighted lines, and a small size for tool output in a Trace.
      card
        card
          row
            text app/api/chat/route.ts
            row
              button "Wrap lines"
              button Download
              button Copy
          text "import { streamText } from \"ai\" export async function POST(request: Request) { const { messages } = await request.json() const result = streamText({ model: \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5\", system: \"You are a helpful assistant. Answer in one short paragraph.\", messages, }) return result.toUIMessageStreamResponse()}"
        text "\"use client\" import * as React from \"react\""
        button "View Code"
      card
        row
          tabs
            tab *pnpm
              text pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add trace
            tab npm
            tab yarn
            tab bun
        button Copy
      h2 Building agentic interfaces#
      text Trace and Code Block join the  we released in June. Together, they cover every part of an agentic interface:
      link "components for chat interfaces" /docs/changelog/2026-06-chat-components
      text holds the thread. It anchors each turn, follows the reply while it streams and loads history without jumping.
      link "Message Scroller" /docs/components/base/message-scroller
      text is one turn, with an avatar, a header and a footer for actions.
      link Message /docs/components/base/message
      text holds what the person sends.
      link Bubble /docs/components/base/bubble
      text shows the steps the agent takes inside a turn.
      link Trace /docs/components/base/trace
      text shows the code, commands and output those steps produce.
      link "Code Block" /docs/components/base/code-block
      text adds notes and dividers between turns.
      link Marker /docs/components/base/marker
      text shows the files that come with a message.
      link Attachment /docs/components/base/attachment
      text asks the person to clarify before the agent continues.
      link Questionnaire /docs/components/base/questionnaire
      h2 AI Elements#
      text are patterns for agentic interfaces built from these components. There is no package to install. Each pattern is a composition you copy into your project, and the code is yours.
      link "AI Elements" /docs/ai-elements
      card
        row
          col
            text New Thread
            text Ask the agent to change your code.
          button "Reset conversation"
        card
        text What should we build?
        text The agent can read your code, run commands and make changes. Press send to start.
        card
          field Message
          row
            button "Add context"
            button Send primary
        text "\"use client\" import * as React from \"react\""
        button "View Code"
      text There are 21 patterns, from  and  to , ,  and . Each page shows how to render the pattern from AI SDK message parts.
      row
        link Chat /docs/ai-elements/base/chat
        link "Prompt Input" /docs/ai-elements/base/prompt-input
        link Reasoning /docs/ai-elements/base/reasoning
        link Tool /docs/ai-elements/base/tool
        link Plan /docs/ai-elements/base/plan
        link Subagent /docs/ai-elements/base/subagent
      text Every page is also available as Markdown, so you can point your coding agent at it:
      card
        text Prompt
        button Copy
        text "Add the Reasoning pattern from https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/ai-elements/base/reasoning.md to my chat."
      h2 Migrating from AI SDK Elements#
      text AI Elements replaces , and we are going to deprecate AI SDK Elements.
      link "AI SDK Elements" https://elements.ai-sdk.dev
      text You do not need to migrate right away. The components you installed from AI SDK Elements were copied into your project, so they keep working.
      text When you are ready, each pattern page has a migration prompt. Paste it into your coding agent, and it rebuilds the wrapper in components/ai-elements on Trace, Code Block and the other shadcn/ui components. Every export keeps its name and props, so the code that uses it does not change.
      text See  for the element that replaces each file, and for the elements we did not rebuild. If you still need one of those,  and we will help you build it.
      link "Migrating from AI SDK Elements" /docs/ai-elements#migrating-from-ai-sdk-elements
      link "open an issue" https://github.com/shadcn-ui/ui/issues/new?template=feature_request.yml
      link "Read the Docs" /docs/ai-elements
      h2 September 2026 - cn
      text Every shadcn component now imports cn from the cn package.
      text For years, every shadcn project started with the same five lines in lib/utils.ts: import clsx, import twMerge, wrap one in the other, export it as cn. It worked, but it meant two dependencies and a helper you had to copy into every project and every registry.
      text We have moved that into a package.  is a drop-in replacement for . It is smaller, faster and ships the same API, so nothing changes at the call site.
      row
        link cn https://github.com/shadcn-ui/cn
        text twMerge(clsx(...))
      card
        button Copy
        text "import { cn } from \"cn\" ;<div className={cn(\"flex items-center\", className)} />"
      h2 What changed#
      text Registry components, blocks and examples import cn from cn instead of @/lib/utils.
      text npx shadcn init installs cn and generates a one-line lib/utils.ts.
      text Every registry item that uses cn declares it as a dependency, so npx shadcn add installs it in projects created before this change.
      h2 lib/utils.ts#
      text The utils registry item still exists. It now re-exports cn so your own code keeps working and you still have a single place for project helpers.
      card
        text lib/utils.ts
        button Copy
        text "export { cn } from \"cn\""
      h2 Existing projects#
      text Nothing breaks. Your lib/utils.ts keeps working and new components install cn next to it. To move the rest of your project over and drop clsx and tailwind-merge, run the migration:
      card
        row
          tabs
            tab *pnpm
              text pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add code-block
            tab npm
            tab yarn
            tab bun
        button Copy
      text It rewrites imports, replaces  calls and removes the old packages when nothing references them anymore. See the  for details.
      text twMerge(clsx(...))
      link "CLI docs" /docs/cli#migrate-cn
      h2 August 2026 - Private GitHub Registries
      text GitHub registries now work with private repositories.
      text In June, we made it possible to turn any public GitHub repository into a registry. Teams asked for the same thing for their internal code: design systems, feature kits, agent rules and conventions that should not be public.
      text You can now install from private repositories. If you can read the repository, the CLI can install from it.
      card
        row
          tabs
            tab *pnpm
              text pnpm dlx shadcn@latest migrate cn
            tab npm
            tab yarn
            tab bun
        button Copy
      h2 Zero configuration#
      text If you are logged in to the GitHub CLI, there is nothing to set up.
      card
        button Copy
        text gh auth login
      text When a repository is not publicly readable, the CLI reads it through gh using your stored credentials. The token stays inside the GitHub CLI. It never enters the shadcn process.
      card
        button Copy
        text ✔ Using gh credentials. ✔ Checking registry. ✔ Created 1 file: - lib/auth.ts
      h2 CI support#
      text Where the GitHub CLI is not installed, set GH_TOKEN or GITHUB_TOKEN:
      card
        button Copy
        text GH_TOKEN=github_pat_xxx npx shadcn@latest add acme/internal-toolkit/auth-kit
      text We recommend a fine-grained personal access token scoped to the repository with Contents: Read-only access.
      h2 Works with every command#
      text Private repositories work with the same commands as public GitHub registries.
      card
        row
          tabs
            tab *pnpm
              text pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add acme/internal-toolkit/auth-kit
            tab npm
            tab yarn
            tab bun
        button Copy
      card
        row
          tabs
            tab *pnpm
              text pnpm dlx shadcn@latest list acme/internal-toolkit
            tab npm
            tab yarn
            tab bun
        button Copy
      card
        row
          tabs
            tab *pnpm
              text pnpm dlx shadcn@latest search acme/internal-toolkit --query auth
            tab npm
            tab yarn
            tab bun
        button Copy
      card
        row
          tabs
            tab *pnpm
              text pnpm dlx shadcn@latest view acme/internal-toolkit/auth-kit
            tab npm
            tab yarn
            tab bun
        button Copy
      h2 How it works#
      text Public repositories are read anonymously, exactly as before. No credentials are used and the GitHub CLI is never invoked.
      text The CLI tries anonymous access first and only uses your credentials when the repository is not publicly readable.
      text Private files are read through GitHub's Contents API, pinned to the resolved commit SHA.
      text A token from GH_TOKEN is only ever sent to api.github.com. Stored GitHub CLI credentials are read by gh, not by the CLI.
      text See the  docs for the full guide.
      link "Private repositories" /docs/registry/github#private-repositories
      h2 August 2026 - Human in the Loop
      text @shadcn/helpers can now mock human-in-the-loop flows for the AI SDK. A scripted conversation can pause for real user input, wait for an approval, and continue with whatever the user decided.
      text Everything streams through the real useChat lifecycle, so your tool cards, approval prompts, and question flows behave exactly as they would in production.
      card
        button Copy
        text "import { createChat } from \"@shadcn/helpers/ai-sdk\" const chat = createChat<ChatMessage>() .user(\"Help me plan the next prototype.\") .assistant(({ writer }) => { writer.text(\"A couple of questions before I start.\") writer.tool(\"askQuestions\", { input: { questions } }) }) .assistant(({ writer, toolCall }) => { writer.text( toolCall?.name === \"askQuestions\" && toolCall.output ? `Starting with ${toolCall.output.answers.direction}.` : \"Starting now.\" ) })"
      text Pass needsApproval to pause behind the user's decision. The scripted output streams after approval, and denial streams automatically.
      card
        button Copy
        text "chat .assistant(({ writer }) => { writer.text(\"That will archive 3 drafts. I need your approval.\") writer.tool(\"archiveDrafts\", { input: { count: 3 }, needsApproval: true, output: { archived: 3 }, }) }) .assistant(({ writer, toolCall }) => { writer.text( toolCall?.approved ? \"Archived 3 drafts.\" : \"Okay, leaving them in place.\" ) })"
      link "Read the Docs" /docs/helpers/ai-sdk#human-in-the-loop
      h2 August 2026 - Questionnaire
      text Today, we're releasing , a new component for multi-step question flows. Use it for agent clarification prompts, onboarding, surveys, intake forms, and configuration.
      link Questionnaire /docs/components/base/questionnaire
      text Questionnaire is available for Base UI, React Aria, and Radix across all eight styles.
      card
        text Questionnaire progress
        text What should the agent build next?
        text Choose a direction or describe another task.
        row
          field "Another agent feature"
        button Next primary
        text "\"use client\" import * as React from \"react\""
        button "View Code"
      h2 Features#
      text Single and multiple selection with native radios and checkboxes.
      text Freeform answers alongside fixed choices.
      text Explicit skipping for optional questions.
      text Previous, next, submit, and custom progress controls.
      text Required and custom validation.
      text Controlled navigation, saved defaults, and conditional questions.
      text Keyboard navigation with optional letter or number shortcuts.
      text Native form serialization and server-rendered collection state.
      text Standalone, Card, and Dialog composition.
      h2 Installation#
      card
        row
          tabs
            tab *pnpm
              text pnpm dlx shadcn@latest registry validate acme/internal-toolkit
            tab npm
            tab yarn
            tab bun
        button Copy
